{"type":"WebSearch","query":""Proposition 5" California (poll OR survey) (2026) (yes) (recall) -PPIC","content":"**California Proposition 5 (November 3, 2026 ballot) is a legislatively referred constitutional amendment titled “Changes Recall Election Process for Statewide Officers.”** It would eliminate the current two-question recall ballot (yes/no on removal plus simultaneous choice of a successor). A “yes” vote means recalls become a single yes/no on ousting the official, with the vacancy then filled later via special election (typically for legislators) or appointment (for most other statewide offices), following existing vacancy rules. Recalled officials could generally run in any subsequent special election (with specifics for governor, where the lieutenant governor would often succeed immediately). A “no” vote keeps the status quo. Fiscal impact is unknown but could involve millions in savings or costs per recall depending on the office. Supporters include groups like the League of Women Voters of California and California Common Cause; it originated from legislation by former Sen. Josh Newman (D).[[1]](https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/5)

Public searches for 2026 polls/surveys on Prop 5 (focusing on “yes” support and the recall change, excluding PPIC) turned up no other independent public polls from outlets like Berkeley IGS, USC, Emerson, SurveyUSA, or similar. Berkeley IGS polls around that time covered other high-profile 2026 propositions (e.g., taxes, voter ID) in their tabulations of likely voter preferences but did not include Prop 5 in the reported results. Ballotpedia and related roundups also list only PPIC data. One forecasting site (On Point Politics) treated it as a toss-up based on available polling averages but relied on the excluded source. Unofficial/early election-night results from the California Secretary of State showed strong “yes” support (around 82% in partial reporting), but that is not a pre-election poll or survey.[[2]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

Prop 5 received relatively low voter interest compared to other 2026 measures (e.g., tax or housing initiatives). For more, see the official voter guide or Ballotpedia’s page on the measure.","citations":["https://quickguidetoprops.sos.ca.gov/propositions/2026-11-03/5","https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county"],"allowed_domains":null}
